WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:06.820
In the first film, one altered gene copy jammed a growth switch on.

2
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.320
Why does one stuck switch rarely make a tumour?

3
00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:15.480
And why can blocking just one switch shrink a tumour with many mutations?

4
00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.900
Let us zoom out from that switch and watch the whole cell.

5
00:00:20.933 --> 00:00:25.473
You might expect a stuck growth switch to make a normal cell divide nonstop.

6
00:00:26.773 --> 00:00:29.453
Surprisingly, the cell usually stops instead.

7
00:00:30.773 --> 00:00:35.653
A healthy cell reads a nonstop growth signal as a dangerous fault, not a normal

8
00:00:35.653 --> 00:00:41.613
order. Internal safety guards called checkpoints catch the fault before the cell

9
00:00:41.613 --> 00:00:42.613
runs away.

10
00:00:43.317 --> 00:00:46.757
One major guard is the checkpoint protein p53.

11
00:00:48.077 --> 00:00:52.197
A nonstop growth signal alarms it much like damaged DNA does.

12
00:00:53.457 --> 00:00:58.237
It halts cell division at once or triggers apoptosis, which is programmed cell

13
00:00:58.237 --> 00:01:04.117
death. Even cells that slip past it still need survival signals to stay alive.

14
00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:08.570
That is why one stuck switch is seldom enough for cancer.

15
00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:14.830
To become a tumour, a cell usually collects several cooperating changes over time.

16
00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:20.670
It may pair a stuck on-switch with a lost off-switch, a broken checkpoint, and

17
00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:21.670
blocked cell death.

18
00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:25.990
Step by step, those combined faults let a tumour grow.

19
00:01:27.333 --> 00:01:31.213
Now picture a tumour that already carries all of those mutations.

20
00:01:32.673 --> 00:01:37.413
Its checkpoint is gone, its off-switch is missing, and one growth switch is jammed

21
00:01:37.413 --> 00:01:43.973
on. Suppose a targeted drug shuts off only that one stuck switch, leaving every

22
00:01:43.973 --> 00:01:45.373
other mutation untouched.

23
00:01:46.853 --> 00:01:48.933
Will the tumour keep growing, or will it shrink?

24
00:01:54.933 --> 00:01:59.853
Often, the tumour shrinks, because its cells have come to lean on that one signal.

25
00:02:01.153 --> 00:02:06.113
Their other mutations stay untouched inside every cell, yet the cells stop dividing

26
00:02:06.113 --> 00:02:10.653
or die. Scientists call this dependence oncogene addiction.

27
00:02:10.653 --> 00:02:17.053
That is why one targeted drug can sometimes shrink a tumour carrying many mutations.

28
00:02:18.283 --> 00:02:20.823
Why does turning off one switch hit so hard?

29
00:02:21.943 --> 00:02:25.283
The exact cause is still debated, and two ideas lead.

30
00:02:26.663 --> 00:02:31.263
First, a stuck pathway keeps feedback brakes high, which may quiet nearby backup

31
00:02:31.263 --> 00:02:37.803
routes. Second, once the signal stops, survival signals may fade faster than death

32
00:02:37.803 --> 00:02:41.563
signals. Either way, the cell tips toward death.

33
00:02:42.583 --> 00:02:48.243
Normal checkpoints guard a cell against a single jammed switch, so cancer usually

34
00:02:48.243 --> 00:02:50.683
requires several cooperating changes.

35
00:02:52.063 --> 00:02:56.763
Yet a tumour built around one loud driver can stay hooked on that single signal.

36
00:02:58.003 --> 00:03:03.283
Put both halves together clearly: tumours usually need several changes, yet often

37
00:03:03.283 --> 00:03:04.783
depend on one stuck switch.
